<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish hussars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as winged hussars, husaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish hussars, or husaria, were heavily armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, active from the sixteenth to the eighteenth century. They are known for the wooden frames bearing feathers, the wings, fixed to their armour. Their disciplined shock charges were decisive in many battles, including the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-hussars.jpg" alt="Polish hussars" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Produced by Warszawa Salon Kulikowskiego · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Straz_hetmanska.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first hussars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> were light cavalry: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> units of exiled Serbian warriors who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, known as Rascians, hired to counter Ottoman cavalry. The oldest reference to hussars in Polish records dates to 1500, when Grand Treasurer Andrzej Kościelecki employed Rascians under the banner of the royal household, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> decreed the first hussar formation in 1503 by hiring three Hungarian banners. Recruitment soon began among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> as well. These early hussars played a fairly minor part in victories such as Orsha in 1514 and Obertyn in 1531.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The winged heavy hussar came with the reforms of King Stephen Báthory, who after his election in 1576 reorganised the hussars of his Royal Guard into a heavy formation armed with a long lance. By the 1590s most Polish hussar units had been reformed on this model and were known as husaria. The battle of Lubiszew in 1577 is taken as the start of their golden age, which lasted until the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683 and included Kircholm in 1605, Klushino in 1610 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Khotyn</a> in 1673. At Klushino the Muscovites and Swedes outnumbered the Commonwealth army five to one and were heavily defeated.</p><p>As infantry firearms became more effective in the 18th century, the heavy charge grew obsolete. After their last engagement, at the Battle of Kliszów in 1702, the hussars were largely given ceremonial roles, and in 1776 a parliamentary decree passed their duties and traditions to the uhlans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most hussars came from the wealthier nobility, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a>. Each towarzysz, or companion, raised his own retinue of two to five similarly armed retainers and servants, and several retinues formed a banner of 30 to 60 lances or more. The companion paid for the arms, armour and horses of himself and his men; only the lance, 4.5 to 6.2 metres long and hollow, came from the King or the banner's owner. Besides it a hussar carried a long thrusting sword, a sabre and pistols.</p><p>The charge began slowly in loose order and closed up as it gathered pace, and it was repeated until the enemy broke, with spare lances brought up in wagons. The armour weighed about 15 kilograms, and the horses, bred from Polish and eastern lines, could carry over 100 kilograms for long distances and still charge. Selling a hussar horse outside the Commonwealth was punishable by death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish hussars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>winged hussars</b> (husaria) were its most celebrated arm: armoured lancers who wore feathered wings that added to an intimidating appearance, and whose disciplined, close-ordered charge proved devastating against infantry and lighter cavalry alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A relief army led by King John III Sobieski of Poland routed the Ottoman forces besieging Vienna, ending with a great charge of <b>Polish hussars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-12">12 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
